    coverageNote: "Read from Lehninger 8 section 8.3, B-copy text layer pp.1078-1122 in full, end to end through all six subheadings plus Worked Examples 8-1/8-2 and Box 8-1, covering A printed pp.278-293. THIS NODE HAS NO CZECH PARTNER AND IS TREATED AS FULL-SCOPE, LIKE SECTION 9b'S UNCLAIMED SECTIONS, PER THE USER'S RULING RECORDED IN HANDOFF SECTION 2e: the section 9e two-minute check (sweep2.py, 2026-08-07, cited in section 2e) found methylation of DNA, chemical synthesis, PCR and sequencing all NO CZECH NODE, and the one apparent match (4.1.5, Odbourávání nukleových kyselin) teaches enzymatic catabolism of nucleic acids to uric acid/urea and beta-alanine -- read again in full while writing this node and confirmed to have no overlap at all with denaturation, damage chemistry, methylation, synthesis or sequencing. THIS NODE'S OWN INDEPENDENT SWEEP, RUN BEFORE WRITING, WIDENED THE KEYWORD NET beyond section 9e's original list (denaturace, tání, teplota tání, hyperchromni, hypochromni, 260 nm, renaturace, hybridizace, PCR, sekvenovani, methylace, metylace, depurinace, deaminace, dimer, mutace, restrikcni, oligonukleotid, Sanger, primer, genom) across all 207 Czech nodes and found exactly ONE genuine near-miss, correctly left alone: Czech 4-1-3-1 (DNA polymerase I) names 'ultraviolet-induced pyrimidine dimers' ONCE, as the example substrate its nick-translation repair activity excises -- a single passing mention of a repair TARGET, not a teaching of the photochemistry that creates the lesion, the same shape as section 13j's 2-2-4 near-miss for L-5-1-1 (a one-sentence mention that stays where it is rather than becoming a join). A second near-miss, 'Sanger', is a false friend rather than an overlap: Czech ch10 cites 'Sanger 1953' three times for Frederick Sanger's EARLIER work determining the primary structure of insulin by chemical (not enzymatic) methods -- the same person, a completely different discovery, decades before the dideoxy sequencing method this node covers; the two are not conflated anywhere in this node. WHAT MAKES THIS NODE UNUSUAL AMONG THE EIGHT WRITTEN SO FAR: with no Czech material to avoid restating, every point below is new to the reader rather than a supplement to something already taught -- the ordinary 'what the Czech node already has' paragraph does not apply here. THE SECTION 5a ORGANIC-CHEMISTRY RULE WAS FLAGGED AS TIGHT FOR THIS TOPIC (phosphoramidite chemical synthesis) AND FOLLOWED THE SECTION 13g METHOD: every chemical step is routed to something visible, measured or clinically consequential. The phosphoramidite cycle itself is described only as far as the book's own OBSERVABLE readout -- the orange DMT protecting group, removed by an acid wash and followed BY EYE / SPECTROPHOTOMETRICALLY, exactly the same technique already used for Edman degradation and ninhydrin in the amino-acid chapters -- and the per-step efficiency is reported as the measured ceiling it sets (70-80 nucleotides routine), not as reaction mechanism. No electronic-structure or resonance argument appears anywhere in the node; UV photoproducts are described by their visible structural consequence (the helix physically kinks) rather than by orbital overlap, and O6-methylguanine's effect is given as its measurable consequence (it cannot base-pair with cytosine) rather than as a mechanism. CITATION METHOD, PER SECTION 16: every A page number below was resolved by DIRECT SEARCH IN A's OWN OCR TEXT (case-sensitive, word-boundary-guarded for figure labels per section 13g's FIGURE 21-1/21-13 lesson), never taken from lehninger_toc.tsv, which section 13h and 13i both found displaced, and never taken from B's page numbers, which are ebook-reflow artefacts. THREE NEW OCR TRAPS SURFACED WHILE RESOLVING THESE CITATIONS, worth recording alongside section 16b's four: (1) A's OCR sometimes breaks the word FIGURE ITSELF, not just its digits -- FIGURE 8-33 exists on A p.283 but renders as 'Fl GU RE 8-33', invisible to a search for the literal string 'FIGURE 8-33'; found only by reading the page directly after the exact-match search returned nothing. (2) BOX 8-1's own header label does not survive OCR as the searchable string 'BOX 8-1' anywhere in the scanned range, although the book's own cross-reference to it ('forensic medicine (Box 8-1)') is present and legible on A p.286; the box's actual content was located instead by searching its opening sentence, 'A Potent Weapon in Forensic Medicine', found intact on A p.288. (3) TABLE 1 (the CODIS locus table inside Box 8-1) likewise does not survive as the string 'TABLE 1', but its content (locus names, chromosome numbers, repeat motifs) was confirmed present and legible on A p.288 by searching column headers ('Chromoso', 'Repeat motif') and one locus name (D3S1358) directly. None of these three is cited by label anywhere below; each is cited by content page instead, resolved the same way. LIMITS: (1) figures were not read as images, only captions and surrounding prose, so structural detail belonging only to a diagram (e.g. the exact geometry of the phosphoramidite cycle in FIGURE 8-32) is not claimed here; (2) this node stops at the end of section 8.3 and says nothing about section 8.4 (Other Functions of Nucleotides, which opens “in addition to their roles as the subunits of nucleic acids, nucleotides have cellular functions as energy carriers” , A pp.293-296), which is a separate lehninger_only_scope.json entry, ruled 'brief' by the user in HANDOFF section 9b and not this node's job; (3) the CRISPR/cloning/library material the user asked to be 'worked properly' sits in sections 9.1/9.2 (HANDOFF section 9b), a different, larger, not-yet-written task, and nothing about gene cloning or genome editing is claimed here even though PCR, which this node does cover in full, is a component of both.",
